---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SupplierIndex from "../../components/SupplierIndex.astro";
import PageContents from "../../components/PageContents.astro";

// The explorer, presets and profile grids all sat on the starter ledger: nine profiles,
// four entity links, no supplier payment rows. Only the payment ledger survives, and it
// is the whole page now rather than the top of it.
const entitySections = [
  { id: "supplier-index", label: "Every supplier" }
];


---

<BaseLayout
  image="/og/entities.png"
  title="Who the Home Office paid for asylum: every supplier"
  description="Every supplier the Home Office paid for asylum since 2010: 366 names, £4.31bn, 16,872 payments, with the years and expense types behind each."
>
  <section class="page-top">
    <section class="page-head">
      <span class="kicker">Entities</span>
      <h1>Who the Home Office paid for asylum, and how much.</h1>
      <p>
        Every supplier the department recorded against an asylum classification since 2010,
        taken from its own published payments over £25,000. What each was paid, how many
        payments, which years, and the largest thing it was billing for.
      </p>
    </section>

    <aside class="page-rail">
      {/*
        This counted the starter ledger: nine profiles, eight sites, nine money rows.
        Printed as the page's headline figures it read as the size of the supplier
        landscape rather than the size of a research stub, and the payment ledger
        directly below counts 366 suppliers. The rail says what the page can and cannot
        show instead of competing with it.
      */}
      <article class="panel panel-pad">
        <span class="tiny">What this page can show</span>
        <p class="rail-copy">
          The Home Office publishes payments to named suppliers, and that is what the
          ledger below lists. It publishes them as supplier names, not as registered
          companies, so this page names who was paid and how much and stops there.
        </p>
        <p class="rail-copy">
          Per-supplier profiles are held back until each name is resolved to a company
          number. A shared name root does not establish a shared legal entity, and on a
          page about accountability that distinction is the whole point.
        </p>
      </article>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <PageContents title="Start here" sections={entitySections} variant="priority" />

  {/*
    The ledger headline totals, then the list itself. /spending/ carries the same headline
    with a twelve-row summary and the money framing around it; this page is the list, and
    the table below it is what makes the two different pages rather than one page twice.
  */}
  <SupplierIndex limit={120} />



</BaseLayout>
